#section(title: "Role and provider authority", lead: "Configuration admits a role before processes or provider effects exist.", body: [
  #answers(title: "Configuration admission", responsibility: [Bind issuer, role, authentication, policy, limits and stores.], interface: [config.new, `service_client` or `resource_server`; pure setters and validate; hellhound.new returns Client or StartError.], interactions: [Validation derives internal settings, transport policy, sealing ring and stable names.], invariants: [No connection or process starts during configuration. Invalid settings produce all detected ConfigErrors.], failure: [Malformed issuer, redirect, scopes, trust, credentials or limits fail before startup.])
  #md-table(3, ([*Role*], [*Required configuration*], [*Admitted operations*],
    [#term("term-relying-party")], [issuer, client id, redirect, authentication], [login, custody, service grants, introspection and local validation],
    [#term("term-service-client")], [issuer, client id, confidential authentication], [client credentials, introspection and validation; no login],
    [#term("term-resource-server")], [issuer; no client identity], [local access-token validation; no grants or introspection],
  ))
  #subsection(title: "Trust and configuration refinements")[
    #points([Issuer is an absolute HTTPS URI with no userinfo, query or fragment. Discovered issuer must equal it exactly. Redirects are absolute, fragment-free HTTPS except explicit loopback policy.], [A per-login redirect is chosen by exact string equality from the default and immutable allowlist. Near matches by port, case, slash or encoding fail before storage. Each address must be registered with the provider.], [Client authentication is PublicClient, secret basic, secret post, secret JWT or private-key JWT. The selected method never falls back silently; service clients require credentials.], [SigningAlgorithm admits RS256, PS256, ES256 or EdDSA, excluding none and HMAC. Keys must fit their algorithm, intended signing use and strength; RSA requires at least 2048 bits and unsupported curves are excluded.], [Secrets and signing/sealing keys are opaque redacted values. Durable stores require an admitted 32-byte sealing key; previous keys only open old records.], [Scope strings follow RFC 6749 bytes and preserve case. OIDC login adds openid; authorization options bound each value to 2 KiB and cannot override reserved parameters.], [Configuration includes response mode, issuer-parameter policy, prompt, max age, login hint, ACR, UI locales, destinations, trust, scopes, durations and store selection. Caller-owned option records extend `default_login` or `default_logout` by record update.])
    #md-table(3, ([*Duration or capacity*], [*Admitted range*], [*Default*],
      [provider request], [1 ms–5 min], [10 s], [startup], [1 ms–10 min], [15 s],
      [store and complete login], [1 ms–10 min each], [5 s / 30 s],
      [pending login lifetime], [1 s–1 day], [10 min],
      [session lifetime], [positive idle ≤ absolute ≤ 1 year], [1 h idle / 12 h absolute],
      [clock tolerance], [0–300 s], [5 s], [refresh margin / wait], [0–1 h / 0–1 min], [30 s / 5 s],
      [provider body], [1 KiB–64 MiB], [1 MiB],
    ))
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Metadata compatibility and signing keys")[
    #answers(title: "Provider cache", responsibility: [Authenticate discovery, admit compatible metadata and maintain signing keys.], interface: [Cached metadata/keys, bounded first-attempt outcome and unknown-key refresh.], interactions: [Native provider parsing uses HTTP Gun; native JOSE selects compatible keys after checking algorithm policy.], invariants: [At most one active fetch per incarnation. Failed reload keeps its last accepted snapshot. Replacement starts empty.], failure: [Unavailable cache is ProviderNotReady; incompatible metadata is typed refusal; malformed keys or metadata never become success.])
    #points([The discovery URI appends /.well-known/openid-configuration to the configured issuer. Discovery and initial JWKS share the startup deadline; background rediscovery permits two provider request timeouts.], [Cache-Control lifetime is clamped to 60 s–24 h, default 1 h. Failed discovery retries with backoff 1–60 s. Failed reload retains the live snapshot.], [An unknown kid can request keys once. The kid is unauthenticated before signature verification, so refreshes are bounded by time alone: one cache incarnation starts at most one unknown-key fetch per 30 s cooldown, however many distinct identifiers arrive. The first unknown kid after startup fetches immediately; within the cooldown an unknown kid is UnknownSigningKey without a request. Concurrent callers share the active fetch. A rotation that coincides with attacker-chosen kids can be refused for up to one cooldown.], [A token naming a kid is checked against the key carrying that kid, else against kid-less keys. A signature failure against kid-less keys for a named kid is an unknown key, so a rotation refreshes instead of reporting bad signature. A token without a kid is checked against all compatible keys and never triggers a refresh on bad signature.], [Unusable JWKs are skipped. X.509 members x5c, x5t, `x5t#S256` and x5u are stripped before gose parsing; key material is verified without certificate-chain trust. TLS trust anchors authenticate transport, not token-key pinning.], [Login requires provider code-flow, authentication, signing and response-mode compatibility. Requiring PAR or signed requests is unsupported. Default PKCE policy requires advertised S256.], [AssumeS256WhenUnadvertised accepts only omission, only for confidential clients. An explicit list without S256 still fails. The same verifier/challenge and nonce remain mandatory; the provider's enforcement cannot be inferred. #adr(3)])
    #behavior(title: "Unknown key identifiers cannot drive repeated key fetches", area: "Key refresh", level: "boundary")[#given[A cache incarnation started an unknown-key fetch less than one cooldown ago.] #when[Tokens naming any number of other unknown kids arrive.] #then[Each is refused as an unknown signing key without a provider request.] #then[After the cooldown elapses, the next unknown kid starts exactly one fetch that concurrent callers share.]]
    #behavior(title: "Unavailable fresh discovery grants no token authority", area: "Provider readiness", level: "boundary")[#given[A replacement cache has no accepted provider snapshot.] #when[The caller requests validation or login.] #then[The operation reports provider unavailability until compatible fresh discovery succeeds.] #then[No earlier startup snapshot becomes authoritative.]]
  ]
])
#section(title: "Login and identity", lead: "The browser holds the sealed pending login; a callback that opens it for this client exchanges the code.", visual: sequence(
  title: "Code login and confirmed custody", participants: (
    (id: "app", label: "Application", shape: "participant"),
    (id: "w", label: "Hellhound", shape: "control"),
    (id: "s", label: "Store", shape: "database"),
    (id: "p", label: "Provider", shape: "boundary"),
  ), steps: (
    seq-msg("app", "w", "begin_login(options)"),
    seq-msg("w", "app", "redirect and sealed login cookie", dashed: true),
    seq-msg("app", "p", "browser authorization"),
    seq-msg("p", "app", "code or denial callback with the login cookie", dashed: true),
    seq-msg("app", "w", "complete_login(request)"),
    seq-alt("cookie opens for this state, issuer and client before expiry", (
      seq-msg("w", "p", "exchange code with sealed verifier/redirect"),
      seq-msg("p", "w", "token response", dashed: true),
      seq-msg("w", "s", "install checked identity and tokens"),
      seq-msg("w", "app", "Session or CustodyUnconfirmed", dashed: true),
    ), otherwise: (seq-msg("w", "app", "typed refusal; nothing sent", dashed: true),)),
    seq-msg("app", "w", "callback_response expires the login cookie"),
  ), caption: [No server state exists between login start and callback. Installation recovery reuses its command and cannot re-enter the provider step.]), body: [
  #answers(title: "Login boundary", responsibility: [Generate independent login material, seal it for the browser, open it at the callback for this client and issue checked identity/session.], interface: [`begin_login`; `login_response`; `complete_login`; `callback_response`; `recover_custody`; identity accessors and `decode_claims`.], interactions: [Callback parser, sealing key ring, native token client, JOSE verifier and custody installation.], invariants: [Login start writes no server state. Exchange requires the browser's sealed login for this state, issuer and client, within its lifetime. Session construction requires custody confirmation.], failure: [Malformed callback, missing, invalid or foreign login cookie, expiry, denial, exchange uncertainty, rejected identity and uncertain installation stay typed.])
  #subsection(title: "Transaction model and lifecycle")[
    #state-type(id: "login-status", title: "Login status", variants: ("vacant", "held", "cleared", "expired"))
    #entity(id: "login", title: "Login transaction", description: [One pending authorization-code exchange, sealed into a cookie that only the starting browser holds.], kind: "aggregate", owner: "Hellhound", lifecycle: "stateful", domain: "OIDC login", tint: "blue")[
      #attribute(id: "status", name: "Status", type: "Login status", provenance: "derived", state-type: "login-status", state-machine: "login-lifecycle")[Derived at the callback from the presence of the cookie, whether it opens and its sealed expiry.]
      #attribute(name: "Binding material", type: "state, nonce, verifier, redirect, issuer, client id", provenance: "derived")[Fresh cryptographic material, the exact selected redirect and the starting client's identity, sealed at creation.]
      #attribute(name: "Lifetime", type: "wall-clock seconds", provenance: "derived")[The sealed expiry, which the callback checks; the cookie's `Max-Age` equals the login lifetime.]
      #relates(cardinality: "n : 1")[Belongs to one configured client and issuer.]
      #relates(cardinality: "1 : 0..1")[An accepted code exchange may produce one checked identity installation.]
    ]
    #state-machine(id: "login-lifecycle", subject: "login", state-field: "status", state-type: "login-status", title: "Browser-held transaction", initial: "vacant", accepting: ("cleared", "expired"), states: ("vacant", "held", "cleared", "expired"), transitions: (("vacant", "held", "login_response sets cookie"), ("held", "cleared", "callback_response expires cookie"), ("held", "expired", "lifetime ends")), caption: [Hellhound keeps no terminal record, so a callback cannot tell a replay from a login it never saw. The provider's single-use authorization code is the replay authority. #adr(26)])
    #points([State, nonce and verifier each use 32 independent CSPRNG bytes. Their base64url representation has 43 characters; S256 derives the challenge from the same verifier.], [`begin_login` reads nothing from the request and writes nothing on the server. It seals the material with the client's key ring; the additional data binds the record kind and the state.], [`login_response` produces 303, Location and no-store and sets the #term("term-login-cookie") `__Host-hellhound_login_` plus 64 bits of the state digest, with Secure, HttpOnly, no Domain, `Path=/`, `Max-Age` equal to the login lifetime, and SameSite Lax for query or None for form post. The `__Host-` prefix stops a sibling subdomain from setting it; a cookie under any other name is ignored. Every pending-login cookie is sent on every request to the host until it expires. Each login has its own cookie, so parallel tabs complete independently. `callback_response` expires the cookie the callback names.], [Callbacks require GET query or form POST of application/x-www-form-urlencoded according to configured mode. Parsing rejects duplicate/ambiguous parameters, malformed encoding, missing state/code, empty code and oversized input: 16 KiB total, 4 KiB per value.], [The callback's state names the cookie. A missing cookie is LoginCookieMissing; a cookie that does not open is LoginCookieInvalid; sealed issuer or client id that differ from the client's are ForeignLogin; a passed sealed expiry is LoginExpired. A present iss equals the configured issuer; absence follows the explicit issuer-parameter policy and advertised requirement. None of these sends a token request.], [Configuration validation bounds the issuer, client id and longest redirect URI to 2 KiB together, so a login cookie stays under the 4 KiB a browser keeps. Without a configured sealing key a callback must reach the node that started the login; a shared key lets any node complete it. #adr(26)])
    #behavior(title: "A callback without its sealed login sends nothing", area: "Login callback", level: "boundary")[#given[A callback names a state.] #when[The request carries no cookie for that state, or the cookie was altered, sealed for another state or sealed under a key the client does not hold.] #then[The callback is refused as LoginCookieMissing or LoginCookieInvalid.] #then[No token request is sent.]]
    #behavior(title: "A login sealed by another client is refused", area: "Login callback", level: "boundary")[#given[Two clients with different issuers or client ids share a sealing key.] #when[A login started at one reaches the other's callback.] #then[The callback is refused as ForeignLogin and no token request is sent.] #then[The login still completes at the client that started it.]]
    #behavior(title: "An expired login sends nothing", area: "Login callback", level: "boundary")[#given[A login cookie whose sealed expiry has passed.] #when[Its callback is completed.] #then[The caller receives LoginExpired and no token request is sent.]]
    #behavior(title: "Login starts cannot refuse other logins", area: "Login start", level: "boundary")[#given[Any number of login starts from anywhere.] #when[Another browser completes its own login.] #then[Its outcome is independent of those starts, because no server state exists for them.]]
    #behavior(title: "A duplicated callback reaches the provider", area: "Login callback", level: "boundary")[#given[A browser sends the same callback with the same cookie more than once before the cookie is cleared.] #when[Each copy is completed.] #then[Each sends a token request with the sealed verifier.] #then[The provider's single-use code admits at most one; the others receive the provider's refusal.]]
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Verification and identity projections")[
    #points([The code request retains configured client authentication, exact redirect, verifier and nonce. Hellhound does not retry an exchange after rejection, timeout, rejected identity or possible send. Proven no-send also requires a new login.], [Verification first rejects unsupported compact/JWE shape, none or non-allowlisted algorithm. It selects algorithm-compatible keys and delegates signature verification to gose; Hellhound performs its stricter claim profile.], [ID token is mandatory for login. Issuer is exact; audience is exactly the client; a present azp matches it; sub, iat and exp are required. Expiry is strict; iat, nbf and max-age authentication checks admit configured tolerance.], [Nonce is mandatory and equals the transaction. A present `at_hash` is checked against access material. Mistyped azp/`at_hash` are rejected; `max_age` requires a nonfuture sufficiently recent `auth_time`.], [VerifiedIdentity stores authenticated issuer/subject and two checked claim sets. The #term("term-login-claims") are those of the login ID token. The #term("term-current-claims") are those of the latest verified ID token, which equal the login claims until a refresh returns one. Authentication time, ACR and AMR read the login claims; every other accessor and `decode_claims` read the current claims. A claim absent from the latest token is absent, so a missing permission claim means no permission. #adr(25)], [Email is optional and is never a stable identity key. Standard profile fields, `email_verified`, `auth_time`, ACR, AMR and caller-decoded claims retain absent values honestly.], [`decode_claims` accepts the caller's ordinary JSON Decoder and returns caller-owned values or DecodeErrors. Decoding a permission claim does not itself authorize a business action.], [One monotonic login deadline is passed through exchange, key refresh and installation. Before exchange expiry is LoginTimedOut; uncertain installation is CustodyUnconfirmed. Cleanup and synchronous observation limits are stated in Lifecycle and limits.])
    #behavior(title: "Uncertain installation recovers without exchange", area: "Login installation", level: "boundary")[#given[Identity was checked and installation acknowledgement is uncertain.] #when[The caller submits the retained custody recovery to its client.] #then[Custody repeats the same installation command and returns its existing confirmed session when known.] #then[No authorization code is exchanged again.]]
  ]
])
#section(title: "Custody and refresh", lead: "Sealed CAS records retain token-generation authority across processes and nodes.", body: [
  #answers(title: "Custody", responsibility: [Own verified session material, expiry, token revision, refresh state and bounded command receipts.], interface: [`restore_session`, `access_token`, refresh, `recover_refresh`, logout; Store is an extension port.], interactions: [Record sealing, CAS transitions, wall clock and native provider calls admitted by dispatch.], invariants: [One fresh refresh dispatch per generation. Publication replaces access and refresh material together. Tombstones prevent late installation resurrection.], failure: [Missing, lost, unreadable and unavailable custody differ; uncertainty is never permission to resend.])
  #subsection(title: "Sealed records and the storage contract")[
    #contract(name: "Sealed-record compare-and-set port", mission: "Let applications persist records without owning OAuth transitions.", tint: "blue", answers: answers-data(responsibility: [Store key, version, `expires_at` and opaque sealed bytes; Hellhound owns interpretation.], interface: [get(key); put(record, WriteCondition); `delete_expired`(now).], interactions: [Captured adapter closures may use a caller-owned database pool. The port holds session rows.], invariants: [Absent inserts only an absent key. AtVersion replaces only the expected version. AtVersionBefore additionally requires a future validity deadline at the protected decision. Written confirms the write; VersionConflict and DeadlineElapsed confirm no write. Version conflict takes precedence over expiry. Delete includes equality at retention expiry.], failure: [StoreUnavailable proves non-action, StoreFull proves refusal, StoreOutcomeUnknown preserves a possibly completed write.]))
    #points([The #term("term-write-condition") is a closed choice: Absent, AtVersion(version), or AtVersionBefore(version, deadline). No guarded insertion or arbitrary predicate is implied. Missing rows fail either versioned condition with VersionConflict. Only the guarded condition may return DeadlineElapsed. Each confirmed result is a #term("term-write-outcome") and AtVersionBefore carries its #term("term-validity-deadline").], [The adapter owns the decision clock: the built-in actor uses its configured Unix-second wall clock; a durable adapter documents its synchronized wall clock and precision. Sample only after exclusive write authority is acquired. Retention and validity are independent. Captured pools remain application-owned.], [Adapters must implement the complete contract without a weaker fallback. Conditional refusals preserve bytes, version and retention. A lost acknowledgement remains StoreOutcomeUnknown; ordinary unguarded writes remain available for valid late publication. #adr(13)])
    #behavior(title: "A conditional refusal leaves the record unchanged", area: "Conditional storage", level: "boundary")[#given[A write requires absence, an expected version or an expected version before a deadline.] #when[The protected decision finds the condition unsatisfied.] #then[The store returns the specific refusal and preserves the record.] #then[A version conflict takes precedence over deadline expiry.]]
    #points([Keys are session: plus the SHA-256 digest of the reference. Plain bearer references, verifiers and tokens never reach the adapter. Store version changes on every accepted record mutation; token revision changes only on publication.], [AES-256-GCM uses a fresh 96-bit nonce; format is version byte, eight-byte key digest id, twelve-byte nonce, sixteen-byte tag and ciphertext. Additional data binds record kind, key and version.], [Current key seals; current and previous keys open. An unreadable or tampered record fails closed. GCM nonce/call budget requires rotation well before about 2^32 seals per key.], [AEAD prevents forgery or moving bytes to another key/version. It does not prevent restoration of a whole older row under its original key/version; applications protect writers, backups and rollback authority.], [In-memory stores use an ephemeral key and epoch-bearing reference. Restart can yield SessionLost; a missing durable reference yields SessionNotFound. A durable store's lifecycle and capacity remain application-owned.], [Every port call executes in an isolated monitored worker and catches adapter exceptions. Timeout/crash maps reads to unavailable and writes to unknown; termination drains the late private reply. Killing that worker cannot revoke a database effect already accepted.], [`check_store` verifies all write conditions, refusal preservation, conflict precedence, byte-exact payload, retention expiry equality and concurrent single-winner writes. Adapter-specific queue/lock tests establish protected clock sampling. Passing those tests does not prove deployment durability or historical rollback prevention. #adr(4)])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Optional PostgreSQL integration")[
    #answers(title: "PostgreSQL record adapter", responsibility: [Implement the complete sealed-record store contract in the optional `hellhound_postgres` package.], interface: [config(pool name), with_schema(Config, name), with_timeouts(Config, statement milliseconds, lock milliseconds), new(Config), initialize(Adapter), records(Adapter).], interactions: [Caller-owned named Pog pool and PostgreSQL schema; core Hellhound has no dependency on this package.], invariants: [Only opaque records are stored. Every callback owns its transaction; no borrowed application transaction is committed. Pool lifetime, sealing keys, capacity and backup protection remain application responsibilities.], failure: [Configuration and unknown formats are refused explicitly. Unconfirmed writes remain unknown; no fallback, retry or automatic pruning is provided.])
    #points([Opaque configuration is built with pure defaults and composable schema/time-limit settings; only new validates the complete value. #adr(14) Configuration admits a quoted schema name of 1–63 bytes, excluding zero bytes and reserved `pg_` names. Positive lock and statement limits are at most 60 seconds, with the lock limit no greater than the statement limit. new is pure. initialize serializes a versioned schema; unknown versions are refused on initialization and every callback.], [A guarded replacement locks the row, compares its version, then samples PostgreSQL `clock_timestamp` at microsecond precision. Exact numeric nanosecond comparison preserves deadline equality refusal; retention seconds/nanoseconds and sealed bytes round-trip without conversion. Missing rows and changed versions take precedence over time.], [Each transaction sets local statement, lock and idle-transaction limits. These limits do not bound pool checkout, network loss or all cleanup; the caller retains Hellhound's store deadline. A timely decision may commit or be acknowledged later. Known pre-write format refusal is unavailable; uncertain database writes are unknown.])
    #behavior(title: "A durable adapter retains opaque records", area: "Conditional storage", level: "boundary")[#given[A caller initialized a supported store and committed a record.] #when[Another adapter reads through the same database.] #then[It receives identical bytes, version and retention time.]]
    #behavior(title: "Unknown storage formats refuse operations", area: "Conditional storage", level: "boundary")[#given[The schema format is unsupported.] #when[A caller initializes or uses an adapter.] #then[The operation refuses access without interpreting or mutating record data.]]
    #points([The public consumer covers the common store conformance suite, fractional expiry, competing writes, protected clock sampling after lock waits, conditional refusals, unknown acknowledgement, client replacement and rotating refresh. It depends on Pog directly, not an application job runner. These receipts establish the tested adapter contract, not production durability, rollback prevention, shutdown completion or security certification.])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Session lifetime and installation")[
    #state-type(id: "session-status", title: "Session custody status", variants: ("vacant", "live", "ended", "expired"))
    #entity(id: "custody", title: "Custody record", description: [The mutable authority behind a session reference.], kind: "aggregate", owner: "Hellhound", lifecycle: "stateful", domain: "Token custody", tint: "blue")[
      #attribute(id: "status", name: "Status", type: "Session custody status", provenance: "derived", state-type: "session-status", state-machine: "session-lifecycle")[Derived at read from the live/tombstone record and lifetime policy.]
      #attribute(name: "Reference and installation", type: "digest key and command identifier", provenance: "derived")[Random 256-bit reference and exact owner-bound installation command.]
      #attribute(name: "Identity and authentication evidence", type: "issuer/subject, claims, nonce, auth_time", provenance: "observed")[Verified original provider evidence, holding the login claims. Refresh neither replaces it nor creates a new authentication event.]
      #attribute(name: "Tokens and token revision", type: "access/refresh/logout hint, expiry, scopes, revision", provenance: "observed")[Accepted provider material; missing expiry remains absent. A code-exchange response without `scope` records the scopes the login requested (RFC 6749 section 5.1), which the pending login retains for that purpose. Publication advances revision. The stored ID token is both the logout hint and the one source of the session's current claims; no second copy of those claims is stored.]
      #attribute(name: "Lifetime and receipts", type: "created_at, last_used_at, bounded command history", provenance: "derived")[Wall-clock absolute/idle expiry and at most eight recent publication receipts.]
      #relates(cardinality: "n : 1")[Each record belongs to one configured provider/custody owner.]
      #relates(cardinality: "1 : 0..1")[At most one outstanding refresh dispatch owns its current generation.]
    ]
    #state-machine(id: "session-lifecycle", subject: "custody", state-field: "status", state-type: "session-status", title: "Session admission and termination", initial: "vacant", accepting: ("live", "ended", "expired"), states: ("vacant", "live", "ended", "expired"), transitions: (("vacant", "live", "confirmed install"), ("live", "ended", "logout"), ("live", "expired", "absolute or idle expiry"), ("ended", "vacant", "retention cleanup"), ("expired", "vacant", "retention cleanup")), caption: [An ended retained installation cannot return to live through recovery. Expired records are denied before sweeper cleanup.])
    #points([Session is an immutable checked view holding client binding, reference, identity and token revision. Custody remains authoritative when a copied view is old. Every session read projects the current claims from the stored ID token; a record without a readable stored ID token projects the login claims and their issue time instead.], [Installation inserts once and retains its command identifier. Recovery uses the same opaque command/reference/material; an earlier accepted command returns its current revision. A conflicting ended installation cannot be reinstalled.], [Install commands older than the login-lifetime recovery horizon fail RecoveryExpired. Logout tombstones preserve provider and install evidence for that horizon; late matching recovery reports RecoveryEnded.], [Session expires at either absolute or idle deadline, with equality invalid. Restore, access, refresh, the bounded claims read and userinfo count as use. Idle touches are coalesced at min(60 s, idle/10); a failed touch does not turn a valid read into a write-confirmed result.], [Record physical retention is at least installation horizon even if live session lifetime is shorter. Sweeper removes records at `expires_at` every 60 s; expiry denies use without waiting for the sweep.], [Session count per identity is unbounded. Applications choose admission/rate/capacity policy; library defaults do not evict an older session to admit a new one. #adr(4)])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Refresh generation state and authority")[
    #state-type(id: "refresh-status", title: "Refresh generation status", variants: ("idle", "outstanding", "orphaned", "quarantined", "revoked"))
    #entity(id: "generation", title: "Refresh generation", description: [The dispatch permission and settlement evidence for one custody token revision.], kind: "entity", owner: "Hellhound custody", lifecycle: "stateful", domain: "Token custody", tint: "blue")[
      #attribute(id: "status", name: "Status", type: "Refresh generation status", provenance: "derived", state-type: "refresh-status", state-machine: "refresh-lifecycle")[Confirmed reservation, settlement or publication determines the current permission.]
      #attribute(name: "Dispatch binding", type: "reference, provider, revision, command, dispatch id", provenance: "derived")[A newly confirmed reservation is the only authority for provider invocation.]
      #attribute(name: "Lease", type: "wall-clock expiry", provenance: "derived")[Request timeout plus two store timeouts plus one second, rounded to seconds.]
      #relates(cardinality: "n : 1")[A generation belongs to one custody record.]
    ]
    #state-machine(id: "refresh-lifecycle", subject: "generation", state-field: "status", state-type: "refresh-status", title: "Refresh permission and settlement", initial: "idle", accepting: ("idle", "orphaned", "quarantined", "revoked"), states: ("idle", "outstanding", "orphaned", "quarantined", "revoked"), transitions: (("idle", "outstanding", "confirmed reservation"), ("outstanding", "idle", "confirmed no-send"), ("outstanding", "idle", "confirmed publication"), ("outstanding", "orphaned", "lease expiry"), ("outstanding", "quarantined", "unknown or invalid result"), ("outstanding", "revoked", "invalid grant"), ("orphaned", "idle", "same dispatch publication"), ("orphaned", "revoked", "same dispatch rejection")), caption: [Lease expiry never releases the predecessor for resend. Its own delayed publication can still install valid new material.])
    #points([reserve binds provider, reference, expected token revision and fresh command. A stale revision returns the current snapshot; busy, quarantined, revoked, missing and unknown reservations never dispatch. CAS conflicts retry at most eight times per transition.], [A confirmed NotSent settlement releases an outstanding generation. A known OAuth refusal such as `invalid_request` may permit later retry, and so does a rate-limit answer (429), which refuses the request before the grant is processed; `invalid_grant` revokes. Any other status, possible send or invalid success quarantines. #adr(25)], [An outstanding lease found expired becomes Orphaned. Late no-send cannot release it; only its own valid publication or definite rejection may settle it. No process-local monitor substitutes for this multi-node lease.], [A refresh response may omit the ID token; the stored one and the current claims then stay as they were. A present ID token must retain issuer, subject, audience, authorized party, original nonce when present and original `auth_time` when present. Once verified it replaces the stored ID token, and with it the current claims, whole and never by merging. Its issue time is not compared with the replaced token's; the bounded claims read enforces age. #adr(25)], [Omitted refresh token or ID-token hint retains prior material; explicit replacement supersedes it. Returned scopes must match the established case-sensitive set; broader scope transitions require a separate profile decision.], [Publication binds dispatch and command, replaces access and refresh material atomically and increments token revision. At most eight recent command/revision pairs permit duplicate publication receipts; old command eviction never authorizes another provider call.], [RefreshUnconfirmed retains exact publication material. `recover_refresh` repeats publication only, and rejects a foreign client. CustodyUnconfirmed and RefreshUnconfirmed have distinct opaque recovery types. #adr(5)])
    #behavior(title: "Possible send withdraws refresh retry permission", area: "Refresh settlement", level: "boundary")[#given[A fresh dispatch authorized one refresh request.] #when[Its provider outcome becomes unknown.] #then[The generation is quarantined and the predecessor is not sent again.] #then[The caller action is reauthentication, rather than an ordinary retry.]]
    #behavior(title: "Publication recovery cannot refresh again", area: "Refresh publication", level: "boundary")[#given[New token material exists but publication was not confirmed.] #when[The caller submits the retained refresh recovery.] #then[Custody attempts the same publication and reports its confirmed current session when recorded.] #then[No provider refresh is dispatched.]]
    #behavior(title: "A refreshed ID token replaces the current claims whole", area: "Refresh publication", level: "boundary")[#given[A live session holds current claims.] #when[A refresh publishes a verified ID token, through access, forced refresh or publication recovery.] #then[Every later session read shows that token's claims and none from an earlier token.] #then[Authentication time, ACR and AMR still show the login's values.]]
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Access operation and competing requests")[
    #points([`access_token` restores the current custody snapshot and returns Access(session, token, `expires_at`, scopes). It refreshes within the default 30-second margin. Missing provider expiry is not fabricated.], [A competing request polls custody with backoff from 20 ms to 250 ms for at most `refresh_wait`, default five seconds. Waiting never creates a second dispatch; another attempt is admitted only after confirmed no-send settlement.], [An old Session uses current custody revision. Forced refresh returns the newer already-published token instead of repeating refresh.], [A margin-triggered refresh with RetryLater may return the still-unexpired current token. Revoked or quarantined outcomes never fall back. Forced refresh never uses that fallback.], [authorize sets Bearer on a caller-owned `gleam_http` request; resource network I/O and resource-request retry remain application-owned. A resource 401 may motivate one forced refresh under application policy.])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Bounded claims read")[
    #answers(title: "Bounded claims read", responsibility: [Return a session's current claims only when they were issued within the caller's bound, renewing them through one shared refresh when they were not.], interface: [`claims_age` builds the checked bound; `current_identity` returns an opaque Current holding the claims, their issue time and the session; `session_identity` stays the unchecked read.], interactions: [Custody read, the ordinary refresh dispatch, and the #term("term-renewal-circuit") held by the provider cache.], invariants: [No provider call when the claims are within the bound. A Current exists only for claims within the bound at the read. Renewal never changes a rotation or quarantine rule.], failure: [A SessionError whose Action names the caller's next step; no error carries claims.])
    #points([The bound is application policy passed with each read. It has no configuration key and no default. Its constructor takes the client the bound is used with, and refuses a value under the floor in the limits table or not greater than that client's clock tolerance, so a bound can never be silently absent or small enough to renew on every request. The #term("term-claims-age") is unrelated to the OIDC `max_age` request parameter.], [The read restores custody first, so a logged-out, expired or foreign session fails as it does for access. Claims pass when their issue time is within the bound and not later than now plus the clock tolerance. A stored issue time further in the future counts as past the bound.], [Past the bound the read refreshes from the current custody revision, whatever revision the caller's Session value holds, and competing readers share that one dispatch through the existing wait. The result is checked again. An omitted ID token is NotReturned. A token just issued yet already past the bound is IssuedTooLongAgo, which a provider clock behind by more than the bound produces. Both ask an operator to act and keep the session, because a new login would end the same way. An unconfirmed publication is returned as the ordinary recovery error and is never recovered inside the call.], [The read has no single time limit, as access has none: each step has its own, which are the refresh wait, the request timeout, and the store timeout on every custody call, of which the reservation makes several when it retries a conflict. The undecided-refresh fallback of access never applies: a read that cannot show claims within the bound returns no claims.], [Current is valid for the request that obtained it. Forced renewal is the ordinary forced refresh. The bound limits the age of the last issued ID token; whether those claims reflect a provider-side change depends on the provider re-evaluating claims on refresh. #adr(25)], [The #term("term-renewal-circuit") guards only this read. While the #term("term-renewal-circuit") of the client's provider cache is open, the read starts no renewal and answers RenewalPaused, a not-sent failure; the circuit's triggers are stated once, in its behaviour below. A session found already quarantined does not open it. Access and forced refresh ignore it, and renewals already in flight finish. The state is per node and per cache incarnation and is lost on restart.])
    #behavior(title: "Claims within the bound cost no provider call", area: "Bounded claims read", level: "boundary")[#given[A live session's current claims were issued within the caller's bound.] #when[The caller reads the current identity.] #then[It receives those claims and their issue time.] #then[No provider request is sent.]]
    #behavior(title: "Competing reads past the bound share one renewal", area: "Bounded claims read", level: "boundary")[#given[A live session's current claims are past the bound.] #when[Several callers read the current identity, with current or older session values.] #then[Exactly one refresh is dispatched.] #then[Every caller receives the renewed claims.]]
    #behavior(title: "A renewal without an ID token returns no claims", area: "Bounded claims read", level: "boundary")[#given[The current claims are past the bound.] #when[The refresh response omits the ID token.] #then[The read fails as not renewed, the caller action is to fix configuration, and the session is kept.] #then[Further reads on the node are paused for the window and send nothing.]]
    #behavior(title: "A just-issued token past the bound is an operator problem", area: "Bounded claims read", level: "boundary")[#given[The provider's clock is behind by more than the bound.] #when[A renewal returns a verified ID token.] #then[The read fails as issued too long ago and the caller action is to fix configuration.]]
    #behavior(title: "A provider fault met by a renewal pauses further renewals", area: "Bounded claims read", level: "boundary")[#given[A renewal dispatched by the bounded read ended with the provider's outcome unknown, with a rate-limit answer, or without claims that satisfy the bound.] #when[Other sessions past the bound are read on the same node within the window.] #then[They fail as paused without a provider request and keep their sessions.] #then[After the window one renewal is the probe, and the rest stay paused until it reports.] #then[Access and forced refresh do not consult the circuit.]]
  ]
])
#section(title: "Resource validation and provider operations", lead: "Checked access claims, service grants and introspection are independent of browser identity.", body: [
  #subsection(title: "Local access-token validation")[
    #answers(title: "Resource validator", responsibility: [Verify one JWT against configured issuer, audience, algorithm, token-type, time and required-scope policy.], interface: [resource.new; pure policy setters; verify returns AccessClaims or TokenError; verifier projects to caller token/result/error types.], interactions: [Uses Client's key cache and JOSE verifier; unknown signing keys can trigger bounded refetch.], invariants: [No per-token provider request on cached keys. Audience mismatch is exposed only after other token validation.], failure: [Rejected, WrongAudience, Forbidden and Unavailable preserve distinct caller response meaning.])
    #md-table(3, ([*Check*], [*Default contract*], [*Explicit variation*],
      [size and shape], [nonempty ≤8 KiB compact signed JWS; JWE refused], [none],
      [algorithm and key], [configured asymmetric allowlist before key lookup], [`with_algorithms`],
      [type], [at+jwt or application/at+jwt], [`allow_any_token_type`],
      [issuer and time], [exact issuer; required strict exp; iat required; nbf/iat tolerance], [client clock tolerance],
      [subject and client], [sub required; `client_id` optional with azp fallback; jti optional], [caller claims decoder],
      [audience], [exact singleton configured audience], [AudienceIncluded membership],
      [scopes], [all configured required scopes present; scope string/list or scp list], [`with_required_scopes`],
    ))
    #points([WrongAudience means an otherwise checked token names another resource; forged, expired or malformed tokens never gain that description. Required scopes are checked after token audience validation.], [AccessClaims exposes issuer, subject, optional client id/JWT id, audiences, scopes and Timestamp expiry/issue time. `decode_claims` preserves the caller's types. It grants no business permission.], [Local JWT validation cannot see immediate token revocation. Short token lifetimes bound that lag; introspection is the explicit availability/network alternative when immediate provider state is needed.], [Relay owns endpoint scope admission and bearer challenges. Its recipe leaves required scopes unset in Hellhound, projects a Principal/Attestation and exhaustively maps ErrorKind through `on_error`. Hellhound has no Relay dependency. #adr(6)])
    #behavior(title: "Unavailable keys do not reject or admit a token", area: "Resource validation", level: "boundary")[#given[The validator cannot obtain authoritative issuer keys.] #when[The caller verifies a bearer token.] #then[The result is Unavailable and no access claims are issued.]]
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Userinfo and service token grants")[
    #points([userinfo obtains the token `access_token` would use and checks response subject against established session identity. JSON and signed JWT responses are supported; signed userinfo need not have exp, but its signature and relevant issuer/audience rules remain checked.], [UserInfo is a separate opaque subject-bound claim view and `decode_userinfo` returns caller-owned data. It cannot be substituted for VerifiedIdentity.], [`client_credentials` validates requested scopes locally, uses exactly configured confidential authentication and returns ClientToken with opaque AccessToken, optional Timestamp expiry and granted scopes. One call performs one grant; no caching or user session is implied.], [The application decides service-token reuse and cache identity. Missing expiry, concurrency, scope/resource policy and request retries remain explicit service decisions. #adr(6)])
    #answers(title: "Provider operation boundaries", responsibility: [Encode grants and userinfo requests and interpret provider results without choosing application resource retries.], interface: [userinfo, `client_credentials` and each operation's typed error/describe function.], interactions: [Provider metadata, selected authentication, transport and JOSE validation.], invariants: [Userinfo subject continuity and configured grant authentication are retained.], failure: [Unsupported endpoint/role, invalid scope, provider refusal, malformed response and session error remain distinguishable.])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Introspection and logout")[
    #points([introspect accepts a raw token, refuses >8 KiB locally and treats empty as inactive without a request. It requires configured credentials and an introspection endpoint.], [Introspection returns ActiveToken(TokenInfo) or InactiveToken; provider/network failure stays Error. exp is strict when present and nbf admits configured tolerance; invalid timing is inactive.], [TokenInfo carries optional subject/client id/time/expiry/type, scopes, audiences and opaque raw claim decoding. Introspection has no resource audience argument: the serving application or Relay must compare audience and permission.], [logout removes custody by reference before provider effects, irrespective of a stale Session revision. It retains a tombstone; missing custody may be treated as already signed out.], [Default logout revokes refresh token, or access token if no refresh token, through RFC 7009 and configured authentication. SkipRevocation is explicit. Failed or unsupported revocation never restores local custody.], [ProviderLogout separately reports redirect, no end-session endpoint or refused endpoint. LogoutRedirect keeps the ID-token hint secret under ordinary inspection; `logout_response` emits 303 and no-store. Post-logout redirect policy is validated.], [Third-party initiation belongs to the public reference RP route: issuer/client selection and target-link handling must be validated by the application. Back/front-channel logout is not provided by that route.])
    #behavior(title: "Provider logout failure cannot restore custody", area: "Session termination", level: "boundary")[#given[Local custody termination was confirmed.] #when[Provider revocation fails.] #then[The returned outcome reports revocation failure and the session remains locally ended.]]
  ]
])
#section(title: "Assertion signing and credential exchange", lead: "Applications compose signed credentials and explicit exchanges independently of OpenID discovery and user custody.", body: [
  #points([A #term("term-signed-assertion") is produced locally. An #term("term-exchange-client") sends it to the configured credential endpoint and admits an #term("term-exchange-token"). Applications retain profile and resource-request authority.])
  #answers(title: "Signed assertion", responsibility: [Admit private keys and produce a signed JWT with exactly configured claims and protected headers.], interface: [assertion.claims and typed setters; with_claim; sign and sign_with_options; to_string; config.signing_key_from_jwk and signing_key_from_pem.], interactions: [Shared key admission and public gose JWS APIs; callers supply profiles and time.], invariants: [No network, implicit clock, issuer, subject, audience, lifetime or JWT id. Signing never creates verified identity or access claims.], failure: [Closed claim, header, size, algorithm and signing errors omit input material.])
  #points([Claims and signed assertions are immutable opaque values whose inspection omits their material. Registered setters replace their own prior value, preserve omitted claims, and use strings, string audiences and whole-second Timestamp values. with_claim accepts arbitrary JSON values but rejects registered names and duplicate custom names; a rejected construction remains rejected through subsequent setters. Empty audience lists are refused.], [RS256 is required. Existing admitted RSA, EC and Ed25519 signing families remain available with an explicit compatible algorithm; none and HMAC are unavailable on this surface. JWK use, key_ops and alg constrain admission and signing. Present alg and kid metadata must be strings. PEM conversion failures are normalized with parse failures. RSA requires at least 2048 bits. PEM admission accepts supported private PKCS#1/PKCS#8 and EC/Ed25519 formats through gose and applies the same refinements. Public-only or unsupported keys fail without their bytes.], [Default sign emits typ JWT and a present key identifier. sign_with_options independently controls optional typ and kid. Protected alg always comes from the explicit algorithm; arbitrary header extensions are excluded. Input payload and resulting compact assertion are bounded to 64 KiB. Applications choose profile-required claims and lifetime, including deliberate backdating.])
  #behavior(title: "Configured assertion material is preserved", area: "Assertion construction", level: "boundary")[#given[A caller supplies admitted signing material and valid claims.] #when[The caller signs with a compatible explicit algorithm.] #then[An independent verifier observes exactly the configured claims and supported protected headers.] #then[Unspecified claims remain absent and nested custom values retain their JSON meaning.]]
  #behavior(title: "Invalid assertion construction fails closed", area: "Assertion construction", level: "boundary")[#given[Claim construction, key admission, header selection or algorithm compatibility is invalid.] #when[The caller attempts to obtain a signed assertion.] #then[The operation returns a typed refusal without exposing credential or claim material.] #then[Later setters cannot erase an earlier construction refusal.]]
  #answers(title: "Credential exchange", responsibility: [Execute one explicitly configured credential request and admit its decoded token result.], interface: [exchange.options, new, supervised, new_request, with_body, with_header, perform; TokenResponse and caller Decoder; token_value and expires_at.], interactions: [A dedicated supervised HTTP Gun pool through the existing transport; no discovery, JWKS, login store or custody.], invariants: [At most one request per perform; no retries, redirects, token cache or refresh policy. Request and returned credential inspection are redacted.], failure: [Configuration, request, transport/send evidence, provider rejection, decoding and empty/unsafe-token failures stay distinct and omit raw responses.])
  #points([The exchange client is an immutable handle with stable pool names. new validates options before allocation of a child process. supervised returns its HTTP pool child; the application starts and stops it through the owning supervisor. Calls before start or after stop fail without creating a replacement pool. Independent clients share no pool. Applications retain captured decoder resources for the call; decoder purity and bounded work are application obligations.], [Options expose system/custom PEM trust, explicit destination policy and host allowlist, a positive request timeout up to ten minutes, a response limit up to 16 MiB, header byte limit up to 1 MiB and header count up to 1000. Defaults are public destinations, system trust, ten seconds, 1 MiB response, 16 KiB headers and 100 headers. Existing HTTPS-only, DNS/TLS, request-size and framing rules apply.], [Requests select a bearer assertion or named form assertion, an empty/form/JSON body, and extra headers. Assertion placement is singular and controlled. Form assertions require empty/form bodies and cannot overwrite an existing field. Duplicate headers/form fields and caller overrides of credential/framing headers are refused before transmission. Default Accept is application/json and may be replaced by one caller header. A caller User-Agent replaces the default rather than duplicating it. Content type is derived from the body.], [Every successful response has a 2xx status and a JSON body decoded by the caller's Decoder(TokenResponse). TokenResponse supplies a token string and optional absolute Timestamp expiry; the caller converts provider-specific relative/absolute expiry fields and handles additional metadata through its decoder. Missing expiry remains absent. Empty or C0/DEL/C1 control-bearing tokens fail. A returned token is opaque and redacted; it proves neither identity, scopes nor business permission. Expiry usability and caching remain caller policy.], [Transport validates headers, encoding, body size and completion before status classification. Its failures take precedence over a non-2xx status, including when an error body is excessive or incomplete. An admitted complete non-2xx response, including a redirect, returns only its observed status; provider response bodies and decoding errors are not retained in diagnostics. Transport failures retain NotSent or MaybeSent evidence and closed transport reasons. Decoder exceptions are normalized without retaining arbitrary terms. Credential material never appears in Hellhound events. The generic API establishes no RFC 7523 or RFC 8693 conformance claim. #adr(21)])
  #behavior(title: "Explicit exchange succeeds without discovery", area: "Credential exchange", level: "boundary")[#given[A supervised exchange client and a valid explicit credential request.] #when[The endpoint accepts the credential and the caller decoder admits its response.] #then[The caller receives a redacted token with exactly the decoded optional expiry.] #then[The endpoint receives one request with the selected credential placement and body.]]
  #behavior(title: "Invalid exchange requests never transmit", area: "Credential exchange", level: "boundary")[#given[A request has conflicting credentials, invalid framing, an insecure destination or an excessive size.] #when[The caller performs the exchange.] #then[It returns a typed refusal with no transmission authority.] #then[No endpoint receives credential material.]]
  #behavior(title: "Exchange failures retain evidence without resend", area: "Credential exchange", level: "boundary")[#given[An exchange is refused, interrupted or returns an inadmissible response.] #when[The call settles.] #then[The caller receives a distinct provider, transport or response failure without secret material.] #then[No redirect or automatic resend occurs, and transport uncertainty remains explicit.]]
  #behavior(title: "Exchange pool lifetime belongs to its supervisor", area: "Exchange lifecycle", level: "boundary")[#given[An exchange handle exists independently of its pool process.] #when[Its owning supervisor starts, restarts or stops the pool.] #then[The same handle addresses the currently supervised pool.] #then[A missing pool never causes an exchange to start an unowned replacement.]]
  #points([Correctness uses the pinned RFC 7520 RS256 vector and independently verifies public API output with test-only erlang-jose and Node crypto. Security cases cover key policy, exact claims, collisions, redaction, transport bounds and uncertain outcomes. Two compiled recipes demonstrate GitHub-style bearer/JSON exchange and OAuth-style form assertion exchange. The OAuth recipe requires a recognized Bearer token_type, accepts it case-insensitively, and distinguishes omitted expiry from malformed or null expiry. External corpus inputs are fetched at an immutable revision with a recorded digest; upstream tests inform cases without becoming runtime dependencies. The test progression is failing behavior, minimal implementation, then refactoring with tests passing.])
])
#section(title: "Transport and observations", lead: "Provider traffic and diagnostics preserve trust, limits and send evidence.", body: [
  #contract(name: "Hellhound to HTTP Gun", mission: "Carry provider operations over bounded verified HTTPS.", tint: "blue", answers: answers-data(responsibility: [Hellhound applies OIDC URL/header/body policy; HTTP Gun owns DNS, destination checks, connection and framing.], interface: [Supervised shared HTTP Gun client and per-request deadline/response limits.], interactions: [One pool per Hellhound client; configured trust anchors, hosts, destination policy and Sinal correlation.], invariants: [No redirects, automatic grant retries or decompression. Requests use selected provider endpoints only.], failure: [NotSubmitted becomes NotSent; possible submission becomes MaybeSent. Typed transport failures omit provider body.]))
  #points([HTTP Gun resolves once per connection, checks every destination address before connection, connects to checked addresses and verifies TLS against the original hostname or IP SAN. Production defaults allow public addresses and system trust; loopback/internal providers need explicit policy.], [Host allowlists constrain hostnames, not ports. Certificate revocation checks are not claimed; deployment egress policy remains a separate owner.], [Hellhound rejects insecure schemes, userinfo/control characters, unsafe headers and requests above 64 KiB. It refuses oversized declared length early and rejects content encoding; provider response default is 1 MiB with 16 KiB head and 100 headers.], [Transport limits bound collected input, not total VM/kernel/TLS heap. Decoded key sets and forwarding add allocations beyond JSON byte size. Concurrent active clients multiply those costs.], [Four malformed responses can map to ReceiveFailed with possible-send evidence rather than finer parse classes. Bare LF in discarded status reason text may be accepted on a close-delimited connection. These existing limitations are retained by #adr(2).], [Unknown foreign shapes and exceptions become sanitized typed failures. Hellhound implements no cryptographic primitive and contains supported foreign boundary failures; no arbitrary formatter turns raw provider terms into diagnostics.])
  #subsection(title: "Typed observations and correlation")[
    #answers(title: "Telemetry", responsibility: [Report completed provider/login/refresh/bounded-read/logout outcomes using closed redacted values.], interface: [hellhound/telemetry descriptors and pure `with_correlation` client view.], interactions: [Sinal emits over native telemetry; correlation is also passed to HTTP Gun.], invariants: [No token, code, state, nonce, verifier, identity, query, body or header enters Hellhound events.], failure: [Observer failure does not authorize a state change; callbacks run synchronously and can add latency.])
    #md-table(3, ([*Descriptor*], [*Emission point*], [*Permitted data*],
      [`http_request`], [provider request returns or fails], [duration, method, host, path, status or evidence/reason, correlation],
      [login], [`complete_login` or `recover_custody` returns], [duration, closed outcome, correlation],
      [refresh], [refresh completes, joins or fails], [duration, closed outcome, whether this refresh replaced the current claims, correlation],
      [logout], [custody has ended], [duration, closed revocation outcome, correlation],
    ))
    #points([`with_correlation` returns a value view and shares all client processes. Caller-supplied correlation is not authentication, a session key or idempotency authority.], [Opaque redaction prevents ordinary inspect output; it does not erase BEAM memory or defend against `sys:get_state`, tracing, crash dumps, malicious trusted callbacks or host administrators. Applications protect these operational surfaces.])
  ]
])
#section(title: "Lifecycle and limits", lead: "Stable handles survive process replacement; admitted effects retain their own cleanup boundary.", visual: diagram(
  altitude: "L3", viewpoint: "runtime", title: "Per-client process ownership", groups: ((id: "tree", label: "Hellhound client supervision", kind: "runtime", tint: "blue"), (id: "fetch", label: "Active fetch", kind: "runtime", tint: "slate"), (id: "app-owner", label: "Application", kind: "runtime", tint: "slate")),
  nodes: (
    (id: "parent", label: "Application parent", kind: "external-system", group: "app-owner"),
    (id: "supervisor", label: "Client supervisor", kind: "component", group: "tree", tint: "blue"),
    (id: "pool", label: "HTTP pool", kind: "component", group: "tree", tint: "blue"),
    (id: "cache", label: "Provider cache", kind: "component", group: "tree", tint: "blue"),
    (id: "sweeper", label: "Sweeper and stores", kind: "component", group: "tree", tint: "blue"),
    (id: "guardian", label: "Fetch guardian", kind: "component", group: "fetch", tint: "blue"),
    (id: "worker", label: "Network worker", kind: "component", group: "fetch", tint: "blue"),
  ), edges: (
    (from: "parent", to: "supervisor", relation: "dependency", label: "restart / stop"),
    (from: "supervisor", to: "pool", relation: "dependency", label: "first child"),
    (from: "supervisor", to: "cache", relation: "dependency", label: "cache incarnation"),
    (from: "supervisor", to: "sweeper", relation: "dependency", label: "cleanup"),
    (from: "cache", to: "guardian", relation: "dependency", label: "one active fetch"),
    (from: "guardian", to: "worker", relation: "dependency", label: "linked worker"),
  ), caption: [The guardian monitors the actual cache and worker. Durable stores are outside this tree and are never joined by startup. The provider cache also holds its incarnation's renewal circuit.]), body: [
  #answers(title: "Client lifecycle", responsibility: [Create and replace only the client's own processes without stale key or name authority.], interface: [new, start, supervised and stop.], interactions: [Application parent, pool, provider cache, sweeper and default stores.], invariants: [Names are allocated once at trusted setup; request strings never become atoms. Live supervisor means AlreadyStarted.], failure: [Startup joins held old children under one deadline; expiry returns StartupTimedOut without takeover. stop is a bounded request, not a completion receipt. Any process holding the client can make that request; the process that called start keeps running.])
  #subsection(title: "Startup and cache replacement")[
    #points([Manual start builds the tree and awaits the cache's first compatible discovery plus keys. First-attempt outcome remains metadata or failure, never a historical key set, so a background recovery cannot hide that initial failure.], [Supervised starts discover in the background. Until ready every relevant operation fails closed. Restart-stable client names do not imply ready metadata or persistent default sessions.], [Whole-tree replacement monitors the actual old pool, cache, sweeper and optional in-memory stores before using their names. Startup cleanup and manual discovery share one monotonic deadline. At most five temporary monitors are removed on exit or expiry.], [A held old child can delay its parent's synchronous startup management up to `startup_timeout`. Existing unrelated client request processes remain independent; no global coordinator serializes them.], [Failed manual start requests shutdown only of the supervisor it created and waits using the remaining budget. Timeout can return while that tree drains, so immediate retry may report AlreadyStarted. #adr(7)])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Fetch ownership and cancellation")[
    #points([Each active fetch has one guardian beside its network worker. The guardian monitors the actual cache and worker and links the worker; cache monitors guardian. There is no idle guardian.], [Completion goes to the cache process subject with guardian identity. Obsolete completion/exit notifications cannot settle newer work or reach a replacement's registered name.], [Cache death terminates the worker even when HTTP or an application telemetry handler blocks. Worker loss releases active state and waiting callers, preserves last accepted snapshot and follows existing retry/reload cadence.], [One active fetch adds one process, three monitors, one link and one forwarding hop. The decoded metadata/key result is copied once more; cost depends on key-set size and is not bounded by JSON bytes alone.], [Readers take the accepted snapshot from a persistent term that the cache incarnation publishes together with its own process identifier, without a message to the cache and without a copy. A published snapshot counts only while that process holds the cache name, so it ends with its incarnation and a replacement is not ready until its own discovery is accepted. The cache publishes before it answers a waiting caller. Publishing an unchanged snapshot costs nothing; a changed one (first discovery, key rotation, restart) costs one global literal collection. The last published term stays in memory after a stop until the next start under the same names.], [Cancellation is process-ownership loss and deadlines rather than a public cancellation-token API. Termination cannot undo a provider request already received. #adr(7)])
    #behavior(title: "Old fetch completion cannot alter a replacement", area: "Cache lifetime", level: "boundary")[#given[A fetch's cache incarnation has ended.] #when[That fetch completes.] #then[Its result cannot become the replacement cache's provider snapshot.]]
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Public defaults and setters")[
    #md-table(3, ([*Operation*], [*Default*], [*Setter*],
      [`start`: discovery, first keys and previous-process cleanup], [15 s], [`config.with_startup_timeout`],
      [`supervised`: background discovery], [retries from 1 s to 60 s], [none],
      [provider request], [10 s; HTTP Gun connect 5 s, pool checkout 5 s, idle read 30 s, idle connection 60 s], [`config.with_request_timeout`],
      [provider response body], [1 MiB; head 16 KiB, 100 headers; no redirects], [`config.with_max_response_bytes`],
      [request body sent], [64 KiB], [none],
      [bearer token introspected or validated], [8 KiB], [none],
      [store call], [5 s], [`config.with_store_timeout`],
      [provider-cache call], [request timeout + 1 s], [none],
      [`complete_login` effect admission], [30 s], [`config.with_login_timeout`],
      [pending login lifetime], [10 min], [`config.with_login_lifetime`],
      [issuer, client id and longest redirect URI together], [2 KiB, so a login cookie fits 4 KiB], [none],
      [callback input and login option values], [16 KiB total and 4 KiB per callback value; 2 KiB per option value], [none],
      [session], [12 h absolute, 1 h idle; sessions per identity unbounded], [`config.with_session_lifetime`],
      [refresh margin before expiry], [30 s], [`config.with_refresh_margin`],
      [wait for another request's refresh], [5 s], [`config.with_refresh_wait`],
      [refresh lease], [request timeout + 2 × store timeout + 1 s], [none],
      [resend after a possible send], [never, for authorization codes and refresh tokens], [none],
      [claims age bound of a checked read], [no default; at least 60 s and greater than the client's clock tolerance], [`hellhound.claims_age`, passed to `current_identity`],
      [renewal circuit after a claims renewal met a provider fault], [60 s per node and cache incarnation], [none],
      [logout revocation], [refresh token revoked under RFC 7009], [`LogoutOptions(revocation:)`],
      [install-recovery horizon and logout tombstones], [the login lifetime], [none],
      [expired-record sweep], [every 60 s], [none],
      [key cache], [`Cache-Control` clamped to 60 s–24 h, else 1 h], [none],
      [unknown-`kid` key refetch], [at most one fetch per 30 s per cache incarnation, regardless of kid; concurrent callers share it], [none],
      [supervisor restarts], [10 in 60 s], [none],
      [clock tolerance for `iat`, `nbf` and `auth_time`; never `exp`], [5 s], [`config.with_clock_tolerance`],
      [ID-token and access-token algorithms], [RS256, PS256, ES256, EdDSA; `none` and HMAC unrepresentable], [`config.with_signing_algorithms`, `resource.with_algorithms`],
      [PKCE], [advertised S256 required], [`config.with_pkce_advertisement`],
      [RFC 9207 `iss`], [required when advertised], [`config.with_issuer_parameter`],
      [destinations and TLS trust], [public addresses, system trust], [`config.with_destinations`, `with_allowed_hosts`, `with_trust`],
      [ID-token audience], [exactly the client], [none],
      [access-token audience and type], [exactly the configured audience; `typ` `at+jwt`], [`resource.with_audience_policy`, `allow_any_token_type`],
      [login cookie], [`__Host-hellhound_login_` plus state digest, `Secure`, `HttpOnly`, `Path=/`, no `Domain`, `Max-Age` the login lifetime, `SameSite=Lax` (`None` for form post)], [none],
      [session storage], [in memory], [`config.with_custody_store`],
      [pending logins], [sealed in browser cookies; none on the server], [`config.with_sealing_key` for several nodes],
      [`client_credentials`], [not cached; one token request per call], [none],
    ))
    #points([This table owns the public defaults. A caller overrides a default only through its setter; no default is read from the environment. #adr(18)])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Bounds and cleanup guarantees")[
    #md-table(3, ([*Boundary*], [*Bound*], [*Expiry or cleanup meaning*],
      [provider request], [10 s default; HTTP Gun connect/checkout 5 s, idle read 30 s, idle connection 60 s], [typed failure retains send evidence; inner bounds can expire earlier],
      [provider-cache call], [request timeout +1 s], [typed not-ready/failure; no fabricated key rejection],
      [complete login], [30 s admission deadline default], [remaining time passed to each effect; cleanup/telemetry caveat below],
      [store call], [5 s worker deadline default], [kill worker, wait DOWN up to 1 s, drain late reply; accepted external effect can remain unknown],
      [refresh wait], [5 s default], [RefreshWaitTimedOut; no dispatch permission],
      [refresh lease], [request +2×store timeout +1 s], [Orphaned quarantine; not resend permission],
      [renewal circuit], [60 s from the renewal that met the fault], [RenewalPaused for the bounded claims read only, then one probe; no dispatch permission is created or withdrawn],
      [stop], [5 s supervisor observation], [Nil on exit or expiry; worker notification can follow supervisor exit],
      [storage sweeping], [60 s cadence], [logical expiry already denies use],
    ))
    #points([The complete-login deadline bounds effect admission and propagated time budgets. A timed-out store's monitor drain can add up to one second, and synchronous telemetry observer work has no Hellhound timeout; the pending verification records this precise return-latency tension.], [stop neither confirms shutdown on wait expiry nor synchronously joins fetch workers or external adapters. Stop a supervised client through its owning parent or it may restart. Applications retain captured resources until their required completion boundary is known. #adr(11)], [Pending logins hold no server capacity: they live in sealed browser cookies. #adr(26) Sessions deliberately have no identity-count cap.])
  ]
])
#section(title: "Failures and caller decisions", lead: "Typed failures preserve phase, authority and effect evidence.", body: [
  #md-table(3, ([*Family*], [*Distinct evidence*], [*Caller handling*],
    [StartError / ConfigError], [invalid config, discovery failure, incompatibility, startup timeout, process failure, already started], [describe safely; fix configuration or apply deployment startup policy],
    [LoginError], [malformed callback, missing/invalid/foreign login cookie, expired login, denial, exchange no-send/unknown, rejected identity, unconfirmed installation], [`login_error_action`],
    [SessionError], [missing/lost/foreign/unreadable/unavailable; absent access/refresh; revoked/refused/rate-limited/no-send/quarantined; wait timeout; unconfirmed publication; claims not renewed (not returned, or issued too long ago); renewal paused], [`session_error_action`],
    [TokenError], [shape/type/algorithm/signature/keys/issuer/time/claims/audience/scopes], [resource.`error_kind` and caller projection],
    [ProviderFailure], [not ready; TransportFailure(evidence, reason); status and closed OAuth code; malformed response; issuer mismatch; unmapped failure], [preserve operation-specific effect meaning],
    [StoreError], [unavailable versus outcome unknown versus full], [adapter must report knowledge honestly],
    [Userinfo / client credentials / introspection / logout], [role/endpoint/option/session/provider failures], [operation-specific typed result and describe function],
  ))
  #md-table(2, ([*Closed Action*], [*Meaning*],
    [Reauthenticate], [Start a new login; never replay a possibly sent code or quarantined refresh token. After a bounded claims read, end the session first.],
    [RetryLater], [No final decision; repeat only the operation whose contract permits it. A bounded claims read denies the request and keeps the session.],
    [Recover], [Submit carried custody or refresh publication recovery; recovery never calls the provider again. A bounded claims read is then repeated, and renews once more when the recovered refresh carried no ID token.],
    [FixConfiguration], [Operator/configuration issue such as foreign owner, unsupported authentication, a stored session that does not open, or a refresh that cannot bring the claims within the claims age bound. The session is kept.],
    [RejectRequest], [Incoming request is invalid and should not be retried.],
  ))
  #points([Error unions and protocol registries may gain variants; Action is the stable closed handling boundary. Use describe functions rather than inspecting raw error material.], [A provider status is not universally definite non-action. Refresh maps only recognized safe refusal evidence to release; possible processing and invalid success quarantine.], [Claims or endpoint error descriptions are never embedded in ordinary errors. Known claim names and missing required scope names are safe typed diagnostics, not raw provider payloads.])
])
#section(title: "Application responsibilities", lead: "Hellhound owns checked evidence and token custody; the application owns every decision that evidence authorizes.", body: [
  #subsection(title: "Routes, origin and browser session")[
    #points([The application mounts login, callback and logout routes at the configured #term("term-public-origin"). Every exact callback URI is registered with the issuer, and per-login redirects come only from the admitted allowlist. A reverse proxy preserves the intended public scheme, host and path.], [The application calls `begin_login` from its login route and passes the configured query or form-post callback request to `complete_login` and `callback_response`. It uses `login_response`, `callback_response` and `logout_response` for Hellhound's response headers. A form-post body is admitted only on the callback route, with a body limit enforced before buffering.], [The #term("term-login-cookie") stays intact until `callback_response` expires it. It carries one pending login and is not the application's authenticated session, so the application chooses its own protected session cookie, lifetime and origin policy and prevents session fixation when login completes.], [The application protects state-changing routes, including logout, with its own CSRF and origin policy, and rate-limits login admission, callbacks and session use. Hellhound's pending-login capacity bounds neither all application traffic nor session count.], [A third-party initiation route validates issuer and client selection and the target link. A URL from that request cannot establish a redirect or permission policy.])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Identity and business permission")[
    #points([The authentication key is the pair of issuer and subject. An email claim is neither a stable identity key nor proof of permission.], [The application defines its users, tenants, roles, resource ownership and approver policy. It projects #term("term-verified-identity") or caller-decoded claims into those types after login and checks permission on every protected operation. #adr(6)], [A permission decision drawn from claims reads them through `current_identity` with the application's own bound, and its authorization helpers take the returned Current. `session_identity` is unchecked and decides no permission. A missing permission claim means no permission. Hellhound bounds the age of that evidence; the application still decides what it permits. #adr(25)], [The application verifies that its provider re-evaluates claims on refresh, keeps a standing check that a removed permission is denied within the bound, and sizes renewal load against its provider's limits. A long-lived connection reads the current identity on each incoming message and closes on a timer once the claims pass the bound, without polling while idle, so the idle lifetime still ends the session.], [A resource server chooses its accepted audience, algorithms, token type and scope policy. Local JWT validation cannot observe immediate provider revocation, so a deployment that needs that authority uses introspection and checks audience and permission on the returned facts.], [Under Relay, Relay owns audience, required-scope and challenge policy for its admission path. Provider unavailability stays distinct from rejected bearer evidence.])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Custody and persistence")[
    #points([The application retains the #term("term-session-reference") in its protected session and restores it for later requests. The reference is a bearer secret. Provider access, refresh and ID-token values stay out of cookies, URLs, logs and ordinary error pages.], [The application chooses in-memory or durable stores before startup. In-memory custody is lost on restart. A durable adapter implements all three write conditions, reports unavailable and unknown outcomes honestly, and passes `testing.check_store` plus deployment-specific race, crash and durability checks.], [The application protects database writes and backups against record rollback, because sealing authenticates a record without establishing monotonic history after whole-row restoration.], [Sealing keys come from trusted secret storage. The current and previous key ring is distributed consistently across nodes and rotated before nonce use becomes unsafe. A destroyed required old key makes its records unreadable.], [Host clocks are synchronized, to within the clock tolerance where claims age is bounded. Durable login and session lifetimes use wall-clock time, operational budgets use monotonic time, and a guarded write samples the adapter's documented clock after exclusive write authority. #adr(13)])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Refresh, retry and logout")[
    #points([The application calls `access_token` for the current usable user token. Concurrent refresh belongs to Hellhound, and callers neither reserve, resend nor repair a rotating token.], [The application handles failures through the closed #term("term-action") projections. Reauthentication follows terminal login failure or refresh quarantine. `Recover` submits the carried custody or publication recovery, which retries publication and never the provider exchange.], [An application that bounds claims age requests a refresh token. On Reauthenticate from the bounded claims read it calls `logout` before redirecting, so a renewal that cannot succeed is not repeated on every request.], [After a resource response, the application decides whether its request is safe to repeat. A forced `refresh` fits only where the application holds that retry authority and does not make a resource effect idempotent. A possibly sent authorization code or refresh token is never retried.], [`client_credentials` performs one grant per call, returns an optional expiry and creates no user session. The application sets its own cache policy for service tokens, including the policy for an absent expiry.], [On logout the application removes its own session, and inspects local custody failure separately from the returned provider revocation and redirect outcomes.])
  ]
  #subsection(title: "Resource lifetime and observability")[
    #points([The application creates client handles once at trusted setup and supervises them under its own parent. A supervised client stops through that parent. A cache restart needs fresh discovery, so a stable handle does not imply provider readiness.], [Adapter connections and other captured resources stay alive until every admitted operation reaches the completion boundary the application requires. `stop` is not a completion receipt for fetch workers or external effects.], [The `complete_login` admission deadline is not a strict return-latency guarantee, because timed-out store cleanup and synchronous telemetry add latency. The application bounds its request handling accordingly.], [The application logs through the `describe_*` functions and typed Sinal events. Correlation carries no authentication authority. Telemetry handlers stay bounded because they run synchronously. Tracing, crash dumps, VM inspection and privileged host access need separate protection.], [Local test-provider trust is used only in tests. Production endpoints require the intended issuer, destination policy and TLS trust. Host allowlists restrict names, so outbound ports need deployment network policy.])
  ]
])
#section(title: "Testing and retained capability scope", lead: "Executable consumers and independent oracles establish specific contracts with explicit evidence limits.", body: [
  #subsection(title: "Test and extension ports")[
    #answers(title: "Public testing support", responsibility: [Provide explicit local issuer behavior and store-conformance checks to consumers.], interface: [hellhound/testing scripted provider, PKI, authorize, token issuance/mutation, request counts and `check_store`.], interactions: [Linked HTTPS listener/state actor using OTP ssl/`public_key` and gose; application routes need no production test hook.], invariants: [Only explicit testing.config/trusting grants loopback and test-root trust. Production defaults never weaken.], failure: [Typed startup/test failures; caller/link lifetime cleans provider processes; fixtures still do not certify security.])
    #points([Typed EmailClaims pairs independently optional email and verification assertions. `with_email_claims` configures one subject; `set_email_claims` changes that subject's future ID tokens and userinfo evidence. None omits the field, while Some(False) preserves explicit false. These assertions do not validate email or confer application permission.], [A subject without an override retains subject-derived email and verified true in ID tokens; userinfo retains subject-derived email without a verification field. Reserved protocol claims, production trust and opaque identity construction remain unchanged. Previously signed tokens and immutable verified identities do not change when fixture settings change. #adr(12)], [Extra claims carry what a real provider adds beside email, such as namespaced roles or profile names. A checked constructor admits any JSON value under a name that is neither a reserved protocol claim nor an email claim, so no extra claim can replace issuer, subject, audience, lifetime, nonce or hash evidence. `with_extra_claims` and `set_extra_claims` script one subject's later ID tokens and userinfo with the same subject scope and immutability as email assertions. #adr(23)])
    #behavior(title: "Scripted email assertions retain absence and subject scope", area: "Public testing support", level: "boundary")[#given[The local test issuer has explicit email assertions for one subject.] #when[It issues identity or userinfo evidence for that subject.] #then[Each assertion is present or absent exactly as configured, including explicit false.] #then[Subjects without overrides retain the issuer's existing default assertions.]]
    #points([A local run fixes the issuer's loopback port and keeps one root in a caller-chosen directory, so a developer trusts it once. The first start writes the root and its owner-only key; later starts reuse the root and sign a fresh leaf. The root is constrained to loopback names with path length 0 and a 90-day validity. A start failure is a typed StartError; the existing start keeps its error type.], [A kept anchor that is unreadable, corrupt, incomplete, expired, exposed or not a loopback-constrained root is refused, never regenerated. Replacement is an explicit call. Starts that share the directory, in any process, take an exclusive lock, so exactly one creates the root and the others load it. A start waits for the lock for a bounded time and then fails as locked. A start killed while holding the lock leaves it behind, so later starts fail as locked until the developer removes it; a start can also time out while a live holder is still working. The developer removes a stale lock only after confirming no start is running against the directory. Hellhound chooses no location and touches no system trust store. The root's key on disk is a residual risk bounded by name constraints for clients that enforce them; this is never for a deployed environment. #adr(24)])
    #behavior(title: "A kept local root is reused or refused, never silently replaced", area: "Public testing support", level: "boundary")[#given[The local test issuer starts with a trust-anchor directory that already holds a root.] #when[The root and key are intact, matching and current.] #then[The issuer presents the same root, and a fixed port gives the same issuer.] #then[Any other state of the directory is a typed start error, and the files are left as they were.]]
    #behavior(title: "A taken fixed port is a typed start error", area: "Public testing support", level: "boundary")[#given[A local test issuer already listens on a fixed loopback port.] #when[Another issuer starts on that port.] #then[The start fails with the port in use, and only loopback is ever served.]]
    #behavior(title: "Reserved names cannot become extra claims", area: "Public testing support", level: "boundary")[#given[A test builds an extra claim.] #when[Its name is a reserved protocol or email claim.] #then[The constructor refuses it with the reserved name.]]
    #behavior(title: "Scripted extra claims reach later evidence for one subject", area: "Public testing support", level: "boundary")[#given[The local test issuer has admitted extra claims for one subject.] #when[It issues identity or userinfo evidence.] #then[That subject's verified identity and userinfo carry the claims, and every protocol claim keeps its computed value.] #then[Subjects without extra claims receive none.]]
    #behavior(title: "Changing fixture assertions preserves prior identity evidence", area: "Public testing support", level: "boundary")[#given[A client already holds checked identity from the local test issuer.] #when[The fixture changes that subject's email assertions.] #then[Later evidence reflects the new assertions.] #then[The identity held earlier and its issuer/subject key remain unchanged.]]
    #points([The provider supplies discovery/JWKS, S256 code login, rotating refresh, service grants, userinfo, introspection, revocation and end-session. It scripts sign-in/refusal, clock skew, audience/key changes and forged tokens. The refresh answer is scripted too: new tokens without an ID token, new tokens with an ID token carrying the subject's claims as scripted at that moment, or a 503 or 429 that leaves the grant untouched.], [Scripted scopes are per subject: configured list replaces requested scopes exactly, including empty or unrequested scopes; no entry preserves requested scopes. One grant feeds token response, JWT and introspection.], [Testing trust exposes PEM for Hellhound and DER for HTTP Gun. Test listener/state are linked to their caller; tests stop supervised fixtures through their parents.], [Compiled consumer exercises common login/access/logout, advanced authentication/transport policy, custom CAS storage and caller-owned claims/errors. It also holds one runnable usage example per topic (supervision, durable stores, callback addresses, resource servers, the local test provider, application decisions and a permission decided from claims of a bounded age), and the README shows their marked regions verbatim. Relay consumer verifies admission/challenge behavior.], [Negative compiler fixtures reject forged opaque authority and interchange of identity, userinfo, introspection, session, access token and recovery phases. Runtime tests establish race, uncertainty, redaction, bounds and process ownership beyond those type checks.])
  ]
